cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If this was useful in your work, please cite it."
title: "In bfloat16 the batch shape moves the importance ratio: measurement, controls, and what removes it"
abstract: "In bfloat16 a transformer gives the same token a different log probability depending on how many sequences share its batch. Across four architectures and eight models the importance ratio that GRPO-style objectives form at their first inner epoch leaves [0.9, 1.1] for 1.8% to 60.0% of tokens, and for 0.00% in fp32. Controls separate padding, batch size and batch membership; layer-wise attribution shows where the divergence enters; fp16 scoring removes it at bf16 cost without overflowing up to 7B; the truncated-sampling term in vLLM's log-probs is measured and removed; and a six-arm, two-seed GRPO run bounds the effect on reward at about a point. The measurement is repeated on three GPUs spanning two hardware architectures, including one with no native bfloat16 tensor cores: the effect is present on all of them, its size is not the same on all of them, and fp32 removes it on all of them."
type: software
